1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,309
Picture a build machine restored
from a disk image made in 2022.

2
00:00:08,349 --> 00:00:12,939
This morning it fetches from GitHub for
the first time since then, and stops.

3
00:00:12,979 --> 00:00:17,990
The message says the remote host identification
has changed, and ends with "Host key

4
00:00:18,030 --> 00:00:22,921
verification failed." A search result offers
a one-line fix: switch the check off.

5
00:00:23,261 --> 00:00:27,896
Your CTO asks: is someone intercepting
our connection, or did GitHub change

6
00:00:27,936 --> 00:00:30,077
something? And what do we never do?

7
00:00:30,417 --> 00:00:34,958
There are exactly two explanations, a
published key rotation or an attack, and a

8
00:00:34,998 --> 00:00:39,768
fixed way to decide between them. And the
one-line fix is the thing you never do: it

9
00:00:39,808 --> 00:00:44,291
makes the error disappear by removing the
check. Hold on to that build machine.

10
00:00:44,331 --> 00:00:46,639
You'll repair it properly before the end.

11
00:00:48,479 --> 00:00:52,314
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

12
00:00:52,354 --> 00:00:56,570
The last two videos covered HTTPS,
where a secret travels: a token.

13
00:00:56,610 --> 00:01:01,589
SSH works on a different principle, and
this video covers it end to end in five

14
00:01:01,629 --> 00:01:06,735
steps. The key pair. The agent that holds
it. The configuration file that decides

15
00:01:06,775 --> 00:01:11,880
which key goes to which host. The host
key, which is the server proving itself to

16
00:01:11,920 --> 00:01:15,882
you. And the test that tells you
who the server thinks you are.

17
00:01:16,222 --> 00:01:21,946
Three replays from labs/ch16. ssh-keys.sh
generates a throwaway key inside the

18
00:01:21,986 --> 00:01:27,782
sandbox. It's volatile, so the key and
its fingerprints differ on every run and

19
00:01:27,822 --> 00:01:33,176
won't match the book. ssh-config.sh
and known-hosts.sh are deterministic.

20
00:01:33,216 --> 00:01:37,018
None of the replays reaches
an agent or the network.

21
00:01:37,058 --> 00:01:40,934
Then the walkthrough of Lab
20.1 in the normal shell.

22
00:01:41,274 --> 00:01:47,959
Labels from the chapter's table. ssh -G,
ssh-add -l, ssh-keygen -l and ssh-keygen -F

23
00:01:47,999 --> 00:01:54,204
are 🟢 SAFE. ssh -T git@github.com is 🟢,
and on first contact may add a line to

24
00:01:54,244 --> 00:02:00,608
known_hosts after asking you. ssh-keygen
-t ed25519 is 🟡 CAUTION: it creates two

25
00:02:00,648 --> 00:02:05,011
files and overwrites an
existing key only after asking.

26
00:02:05,051 --> 00:02:11,176
ssh-add is 🟡. ssh-keygen -R is 🟡: it
removes a host's lines from known_hosts,

27
00:02:11,216 --> 00:02:16,380
keeping a .old copy. And
StrictHostKeyChecking no is 🔴 DANGEROUS.

28
00:02:18,220 --> 00:02:19,632
After this video you can:

29
00:02:19,972 --> 00:02:23,741
Generate a key pair and say what
each half is for and where it goes.

30
00:02:24,081 --> 00:02:27,326
Explain what the agent holds and
what the passphrase protects.

31
00:02:27,666 --> 00:02:35,860
Write a ~/.ssh/config entry with a host
alias and show what SSH resolves it to.

32
00:02:36,200 --> 00:02:40,771
Verify GitHub's host key against the
published fingerprints and handle a changed

33
00:02:40,811 --> 00:02:41,002
key.

34
00:02:41,342 --> 00:02:45,232
Test the connection and
use SSH over port 443.

35
00:02:47,072 --> 00:02:51,682
In one sentence: with SSH you prove who
you are by signing a challenge with a

36
00:02:51,722 --> 00:02:56,331
private key that never leaves your
machine. GitHub holds only the public key.

37
00:02:56,671 --> 00:03:01,979
The key pair. ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C
"your_email@example.com" is the command

38
00:03:02,019 --> 00:03:07,464
GitHub documents. It writes two files:
the private key, and a .pub file with one

39
00:03:07,504 --> 00:03:11,646
line, type, key and comment,
that you upload to your account.

40
00:03:11,686 --> 00:03:16,103
For hardware security keys the
types are ed25519-sk and ecdsa-sk.

41
00:03:16,143 --> 00:03:21,520
When you add a key to GitHub you choose
whether it's an authentication key or a

42
00:03:21,560 --> 00:03:25,496
signing key. To use one key
for both, you upload it twice.

43
00:03:25,536 --> 00:03:29,747
And GitHub deletes SSH keys that
haven't been used for a year.

44
00:03:30,087 --> 00:03:33,903
A fingerprint is a hash of the public
key, short enough to compare by eye.

45
00:03:34,243 --> 00:03:40,782
Inside .git: nothing. Keys live under ~/.ssh,
and which key is offered is decided by

46
00:03:40,822 --> 00:03:45,638
ssh, unless core.sshCommand or
GIT_SSH_COMMAND says otherwise.

47
00:03:45,978 --> 00:03:50,980
The agent. A passphrase encrypts the private
key file, so that a copy of the file is

48
00:03:51,020 --> 00:03:56,023
useless without it. But a passphrase that
must be typed for every fetch gets removed

49
00:03:56,063 --> 00:04:00,885
within a week. The agent is the remedy: a
background process that holds decrypted

50
00:04:00,925 --> 00:04:05,747
keys in memory and signs on request, so
that ssh never needs the passphrase again

51
00:04:05,787 --> 00:04:09,409
during the session and no program
reads the private key file.

52
00:04:09,749 --> 00:04:14,944
ssh-add PATH gives a key to the agent.
ssh-add -l lists the keys it holds, and its

53
00:04:14,984 --> 00:04:18,391
exit status distinguishes
three states. 0 with a list.

54
00:04:18,431 --> 00:04:23,052
1 with "The agent has no identities."
And 2 when no agent can be reached.

55
00:04:23,092 --> 00:04:26,052
macOS provides an agent
for your login session.

56
00:04:26,392 --> 00:04:31,757
GitHub's guide adds a Host github.com block
with AddKeysToAgent yes and UseKeychain

57
00:04:31,797 --> 00:04:36,902
yes, and ssh-add --apple-use-keychain,
which stores the passphrase in the login

58
00:04:36,942 --> 00:04:40,809
keychain so that the agent can
load the key after a restart.

59
00:04:40,849 --> 00:04:44,195
UseKeychain exists only in
Apple's build of OpenSSH.

60
00:04:44,535 --> 00:04:50,055
Try it now, thirty seconds, in your normal
shell. Run ssh-add -l and decide which of

61
00:04:50,095 --> 00:04:54,225
the three states you're in: a
list, no identities, or no agent.

62
00:04:54,265 --> 00:04:54,887
I'll wait.

63
00:04:59,727 --> 00:05:02,969
Whichever you got, you now
know what your agent holds.

64
00:05:03,009 --> 00:05:06,312
That's the first thing to
check when SSH surprises you.

65
00:05:06,652 --> 00:05:12,654
You meet the third state, no agent, under
sudo, in cron jobs and in containers: they

66
00:05:12,694 --> 00:05:18,121
don't inherit SSH_AUTH_SOCK, the variable
that tells ssh where the agent is.

67
00:05:18,461 --> 00:05:22,492
One warning: agent forwarding lets a
remote machine use your local agent.

68
00:05:22,532 --> 00:05:27,065
The manual says it "should be enabled with
caution": whoever controls that machine

69
00:05:27,105 --> 00:05:29,630
can use your identity
while you are connected.

70
00:05:29,970 --> 00:05:35,497
Quick quiz. When you connect to GitHub over
SSH, which user name does ssh send: your

71
00:05:35,537 --> 00:05:39,340
account name, your email address,
or git? Say it out loud.

72
00:05:44,180 --> 00:05:50,519
The configuration file. In one sentence:
~/.ssh/config maps the host name that Git

73
00:05:50,559 --> 00:05:56,587
hands to ssh onto a real host, a user,
a port and a key, and ssh -G prints the

74
00:05:56,627 --> 00:05:59,854
result of that mapping without connecting.

75
00:06:00,194 --> 00:06:05,572
The file is a list of Host blocks. For each
setting, the first value obtained wins,

76
00:06:05,612 --> 00:06:09,227
so specific blocks go first
and Host * defaults go last.

77
00:06:09,267 --> 00:06:14,579
The keywords that matter for GitHub. Host
is the name or pattern that appears in a

78
00:06:14,619 --> 00:06:17,255
URL: github.com, or an alias of your own.

79
00:06:17,595 --> 00:06:22,358
HostName is the real host. User is
always git, and that's the quiz answer.

80
00:06:22,398 --> 00:06:27,485
IdentityFile is a private key to offer.
IdentitiesOnly yes means offer only the

81
00:06:27,525 --> 00:06:30,860
configured files, even if
the agent holds more keys.

82
00:06:30,900 --> 00:06:36,051
Your GitHub account name appears nowhere.
GitHub identifies you by which key was

83
00:06:36,091 --> 00:06:36,635
accepted.

84
00:06:36,975 --> 00:06:42,428
Git's part is small: it splits the
URL and runs ssh with what it found.

85
00:06:42,468 --> 00:06:44,827
Git doesn't read ~/.ssh/config.

86
00:06:45,167 --> 00:06:51,050
Host keys. In one sentence: before you
prove who you are, the server proves who it

87
00:06:51,090 --> 00:06:56,611
is with its host key, and ssh compares
that key with the one it remembered in

88
00:06:56,651 --> 00:06:57,984
~/.ssh/known_hosts.

89
00:06:58,324 --> 00:07:02,980
Without this check, anyone on the network
path could pose as GitHub, accept your

90
00:07:03,020 --> 00:07:07,384
connection and relay it. On the first
connection ssh shows the server key's

91
00:07:07,424 --> 00:07:12,198
fingerprint and asks whether to continue.
Your answer is the whole security of the

92
00:07:12,238 --> 00:07:15,779
scheme, so compare it with the
fingerprints GitHub publishes.

93
00:07:15,819 --> 00:07:20,710
GitHub also publishes the three known_hosts
lines, so you can install them and never

94
00:07:20,750 --> 00:07:21,239
be asked.

95
00:07:21,579 --> 00:07:26,446
A changed host key has two explanations.
One: a published rotation.

96
00:07:26,486 --> 00:07:32,159
GitHub replaced its RSA host key on the
twenty-fourth of March 2023, after the

97
00:07:32,199 --> 00:07:36,187
private key was briefly exposed
in a public repository.

98
00:07:36,227 --> 00:07:41,313
The ECDSA and Ed25519 keys didn't
change. Two: someone is in the path.

99
00:07:41,653 --> 00:07:46,599
How do you decide? GitHub states that a
host key change "will be announced on the

100
00:07:46,639 --> 00:07:51,584
GitHub Blog". So you find the announcement
first, and you compare the fingerprint

101
00:07:51,624 --> 00:07:56,570
with the published ones. Only then do you
remove the old lines with ssh-keygen -R

102
00:07:56,610 --> 00:07:59,216
github.com and install
the published lines.

103
00:07:59,556 --> 00:08:03,955
The 🔴 item: StrictHostKeyChecking no,
and deleting known_hosts wholesale.

104
00:08:03,995 --> 00:08:09,002
What it changes: ssh stops verifying the
server. What it can destroy: the guarantee

105
00:08:09,042 --> 00:08:13,866
that you're talking to GitHub, and with
it the confidentiality of everything you

106
00:08:13,906 --> 00:08:18,609
push. There's no preview and no recovery
for a connection already intercepted.

107
00:08:18,649 --> 00:08:23,291
It isn't appropriate. On a CI runner,
the machine that runs an automated job,

108
00:08:23,331 --> 00:08:25,480
install the published lines instead.

109
00:08:25,820 --> 00:08:29,843
Testing. ssh -T git@github.com
authenticates and runs nothing.

110
00:08:29,883 --> 00:08:35,216
GitHub's documented answer is "Hi USERNAME!
You've successfully authenticated, but

111
00:08:35,256 --> 00:08:40,589
GitHub does not provide shell access.",
and the command exits with status 1, which

112
00:08:40,629 --> 00:08:45,897
is expected. The name in the greeting is
the answer to "who does the server say I

113
00:08:45,937 --> 00:08:50,615
am", and it is the first thing to read
when a repository is "not found".

114
00:08:50,655 --> 00:08:56,119
ssh -vT adds the client's reasoning: which
configuration files were read, which host

115
00:08:56,159 --> 00:08:59,330
and port were contacted,
which keys were offered.

116
00:08:59,670 --> 00:09:05,099
Some networks block port 22. GitHub accepts
SSH on port 443 of a different host

117
00:09:05,139 --> 00:09:10,776
name, ssh.github.com. GitHub's page notes
that the first connection asks about the

118
00:09:10,816 --> 00:09:16,246
host key again, under that other name,
and that this is not available on GitHub

119
00:09:16,286 --> 00:09:21,854
Enterprise Server. The textbook did not
verify the host-key behaviour beyond that

120
00:09:21,894 --> 00:09:22,200
page.

121
00:09:24,040 --> 00:09:27,611
The textbook's analogy: a signet
ring and its wax impression.

122
00:09:27,651 --> 00:09:30,807
You give everyone a sample
impression, the public key.

123
00:09:30,847 --> 00:09:35,247
Only the ring, the private key, can
make a new one, and anyone can compare.

124
00:09:35,587 --> 00:09:40,434
The analogy breaks because a wax seal can
be copied from a sample, and a private key

125
00:09:40,474 --> 00:09:42,587
can't be derived from the public key.

126
00:09:42,926 --> 00:09:46,195
Add the second ring to the
picture: the server has one too.

127
00:09:46,235 --> 00:09:50,569
An SSH connection is two proofs. First
the server shows its impression and you

128
00:09:50,609 --> 00:09:54,888
compare it with the sample in known_hosts.
Then you show yours and the server

129
00:09:54,928 --> 00:09:59,430
compares it with the samples in your
account. The hook of this video is the first

130
00:09:59,470 --> 00:10:03,019
proof failing. Most "permission
denied" problems are the second.

131
00:10:04,859 --> 00:10:09,021
On the left, the private key stays,
and the agent holds it decrypted.

132
00:10:09,061 --> 00:10:13,832
The public half was uploaded once and is
attached to your account on the right.

133
00:10:13,872 --> 00:10:17,608
On the left again, known_hosts
holds GitHub's public host key.

134
00:10:17,948 --> 00:10:22,855
Two arrows cross. First from right to left:
the server proves itself, and your ssh

135
00:10:22,895 --> 00:10:27,742
checks that against known_hosts. Then from
left to right: a challenge signed with

136
00:10:27,782 --> 00:10:31,422
your private key. Nothing secret
travels in either direction.

137
00:10:31,762 --> 00:10:35,742
Four layers. Git splits the URL.
ssh applies its configuration.

138
00:10:35,782 --> 00:10:40,018
The host key is checked. And GitHub
authenticates, then authorizes.

139
00:10:44,358 --> 00:10:49,507
Two files. The private file is readable by
its owner only, and ssh ignores a private

140
00:10:49,547 --> 00:10:51,483
key file that others can access.

141
00:10:51,823 --> 00:10:55,028
The public half: one line
of type, key and comment.

142
00:10:55,068 --> 00:11:00,309
Below it the fingerprint in the SHA256 form,
which is the form GitHub lists in your

143
00:11:00,349 --> 00:11:05,082
account, and in the older MD5 form with
colons that some tools still print.

144
00:11:05,422 --> 00:11:08,893
Suppose you lose the .pub
file. What have you lost?

145
00:11:08,933 --> 00:11:10,752
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

146
00:11:15,592 --> 00:11:20,062
Nothing: ssh-keygen -y recomputes
the public key from the private one.

147
00:11:20,402 --> 00:11:25,022
A passphrase is set, and a wrong guess
fails: "incorrect passphrase supplied to

148
00:11:25,062 --> 00:11:29,269
decrypt private key". On your machine
ssh-keygen asks for the passphrase

149
00:11:29,309 --> 00:11:34,105
interactively. It never belongs on a command
line. The replay does it only because

150
00:11:34,145 --> 00:11:34,931
it can't type.

151
00:11:45,771 --> 00:11:51,103
Four blocks: a personal identity for
github.com, a work identity behind the alias

152
00:11:51,143 --> 00:11:54,950
github-work, the port-443
fallback, and the defaults last.

153
00:11:55,290 --> 00:12:00,042
The alias github-work. Which host will
ssh connect to, and with which key?

154
00:12:00,082 --> 00:12:01,402
Make your prediction.

155
00:12:15,842 --> 00:12:19,517
The same host, github.com,
as a different key holder.

156
00:12:19,557 --> 00:12:25,405
Nothing was contacted: -G evaluates the Host
blocks, prints the settings, and exits.

157
00:12:30,545 --> 00:12:36,320
The port-443 block resolves
to ssh.github.com, port 443.

158
00:12:36,660 --> 00:12:40,831
A user written in the command or in
the URL beats User git in the file.

159
00:12:40,871 --> 00:12:44,804
That's how a well-meant USERNAME@github.com
breaks a working setup.

160
00:12:45,144 --> 00:12:50,668
Now the same file with a Host * block moved
to the top, containing a default user

161
00:12:50,708 --> 00:12:55,132
and an old key. What does github.com
resolve to? Say it out loud.

162
00:12:59,972 --> 00:13:04,895
User deploy won because it was obtained
first, and an old RSA key is now offered

163
00:13:04,935 --> 00:13:09,796
before the right one. On GitHub this
configuration fails with Permission denied

164
00:13:09,836 --> 00:13:14,015
(publickey), and the only place the
cause is visible is this output.

165
00:13:14,355 --> 00:13:18,363
A stand-in for ssh prints the
arguments Git gives it and exits.

166
00:13:18,403 --> 00:13:23,569
Git passed the alias github-work untouched.
Git doesn't know that the alias means

167
00:13:23,609 --> 00:13:24,276
github.com.

168
00:13:31,116 --> 00:13:36,080
The three lines GitHub publishes, copied
for the course on the second of October

169
00:13:36,120 --> 00:13:41,335
2026, and their fingerprints computed locally.
Compare these three with the table in

170
00:13:41,375 --> 00:13:45,026
section 16.11 and with GitHub's
page on the day you record.

171
00:13:45,366 --> 00:13:49,756
ssh-keygen -F answers "what do
I have on file for this host".

172
00:13:49,796 --> 00:13:55,494
Three entries for github.com, and none
for ssh.github.com, which is a different

173
00:13:55,534 --> 00:13:55,857
name.

174
00:13:56,197 --> 00:14:00,805
A different key filed under the name
github.com: what a stale or forged entry looks

175
00:14:00,845 --> 00:14:04,781
like. It looks like any other line.
Only the fingerprint gives it away.

176
00:14:05,121 --> 00:14:09,598
Here's that check as a picture. On the
left, the key GitHub presents, and its

177
00:14:09,638 --> 00:14:10,302
fingerprint.

178
00:14:10,642 --> 00:14:15,444
On the right, the key on file. A fingerprint
is a hash of the key, so the same key

179
00:14:15,484 --> 00:14:17,039
gives the same fingerprint.

180
00:14:17,379 --> 00:14:21,852
With the stale line on file, the two no
longer match, and a connection to the real

181
00:14:21,892 --> 00:14:25,980
GitHub fails with "Host key verification
failed." That's the check working.

182
00:14:26,320 --> 00:14:31,374
The repair: remove the lines for the host,
which keeps a .old copy, then add the

183
00:14:31,414 --> 00:14:32,330
published ones.

184
00:14:32,670 --> 00:14:39,321
Narrate each step by what it changes.
ssh-keygen asks for a file and a passphrase

185
00:14:39,361 --> 00:14:46,261
and writes two files under ~/.ssh. If
id_ed25519 already exists, choose another file

186
00:14:46,301 --> 00:14:53,118
name. Between the second and third commands
the lab has you add the Host github.com

187
00:14:53,158 --> 00:14:56,422
block to ~/.ssh/config with your editor.

188
00:14:56,762 --> 00:15:01,290
ssh-add gives the key to the agent
and the passphrase to the keychain.

189
00:15:01,330 --> 00:15:04,879
gh ssh-key add attaches the
public key to your account.

190
00:15:04,919 --> 00:15:10,361
If it refuses for a missing scope, the lab
gives the web form: in your settings, the

191
00:15:10,401 --> 00:15:14,407
page for SSH and GPG keys, a new
key of type "authentication".

192
00:15:14,747 --> 00:15:19,980
On the first ssh -T, stop at the fingerprint
prompt and compare before you answer.

193
00:15:20,020 --> 00:15:25,125
The documentation says the greeting names
your account and the exit status is 1.

194
00:15:26,965 --> 00:15:28,613
Five mistakes to watch for.

195
00:15:28,953 --> 00:15:32,234
Silencing a host key error
with StrictHostKeyChecking no.

196
00:15:32,274 --> 00:15:37,070
Root cause: the error is the check working;
switching it off removes the only proof

197
00:15:37,110 --> 00:15:38,585
that the server is GitHub.

198
00:15:38,925 --> 00:15:43,190
Writing your account name into the
URL, as in USERNAME@github.com.

199
00:15:43,230 --> 00:15:48,474
Root cause: all connections are made as
the user git; a user in the URL overrides

200
00:15:48,514 --> 00:15:49,648
the configuration.

201
00:15:49,988 --> 00:15:54,084
Putting Host * defaults at
the top of ~/.ssh/config.

202
00:15:54,124 --> 00:15:58,777
Root cause: for each setting
the first value obtained wins.

203
00:15:59,117 --> 00:16:05,505
Expecting SSH to work under sudo, in cron
or in a container because it works in your

204
00:16:05,545 --> 00:16:11,781
terminal. Root cause: those environments
do not inherit SSH_AUTH_SOCK, so no agent

205
00:16:11,821 --> 00:16:12,929
can be reached.

206
00:16:13,269 --> 00:16:18,213
Copying one private key to several machines.
Root cause: a lost machine then forces

207
00:16:18,253 --> 00:16:22,956
revocation everywhere at once; one key
per machine limits that to one deletion.

208
00:16:24,796 --> 00:16:28,152
Now, out of the lab. The
build machine from the hook.

209
00:16:28,192 --> 00:16:31,419
The engineer doesn't touch
the configuration first.

210
00:16:31,459 --> 00:16:36,032
She reads the fingerprint in the error,
opens GitHub's page of published

211
00:16:36,072 --> 00:16:41,413
fingerprints from another machine, and
searches the GitHub Blog for an announcement.

212
00:16:41,453 --> 00:16:46,794
The machine's known_hosts was written in
2022. It holds the RSA host key that GitHub

213
00:16:46,834 --> 00:16:52,047
replaced on the twenty-fourth of March
2023. The published RSA fingerprint matches

214
00:16:52,087 --> 00:16:56,468
what the server now presents.
Explanation one: a rotation, announced.

215
00:16:56,508 --> 00:17:01,144
She runs ssh-keygen -R github.com and
installs the three published lines.

216
00:17:01,484 --> 00:17:05,817
Had the fingerprint matched nothing GitHub
publishes, the machine would have been

217
00:17:05,857 --> 00:17:09,002
taken off that network and the
incident handed to security.

218
00:17:09,342 --> 00:17:15,174
A second habit from the same team: a support
ticket that says "SSH is broken since

219
00:17:15,214 --> 00:17:20,975
yesterday" gets one request before anything
else, the output of ssh -G github.com

220
00:17:21,015 --> 00:17:24,770
filtered to host name, user,
port and identity files.

221
00:17:24,810 --> 00:17:30,355
It shows a corporate tool that prepended
a Host * block, an alias that someone

222
00:17:30,395 --> 00:17:34,867
shadowed, or a port override, in
less time than reading ssh -v.

223
00:17:36,707 --> 00:17:40,657
Your turn. Do Lab 20.1, "SSH
setup and verification", in

224
00:17:40,697 --> 00:17:46,571
lab-manual/m20-authentication-ssh.md. Part
A is a rehearsal in the lab shell with a

225
00:17:46,611 --> 00:17:51,203
throwaway key, and Part B is the
real setup in your normal shell.

226
00:17:51,243 --> 00:17:56,476
Before each ssh -G, predict the host
name, the user and the identity file.

227
00:17:56,516 --> 00:18:01,250
Before your first connection, have
the published fingerprints open.

228
00:18:01,590 --> 00:18:07,852
The challenge is Exercise 20.4,
"Permission denied (publickey)", in

229
00:18:07,892 --> 00:18:10,486
exercises/m19-m25-github.md.

230
00:18:12,326 --> 00:18:15,557
Question 233 of the CTO question bank:

231
00:18:17,897 --> 00:18:22,471
Answer out loud. A strong answer names
both explanations without ranking one as

232
00:18:22,511 --> 00:18:27,377
"probably", and bases the decision on evidence
from outside the affected connection.

233
00:18:27,417 --> 00:18:30,881
It knows the documented rotation
and its date as an example.

234
00:18:30,921 --> 00:18:35,495
It gives the repair commands in order,
and it's unambiguous about the forbidden

235
00:18:35,535 --> 00:18:37,364
shortcut and why it's forbidden.

236
00:18:39,204 --> 00:18:40,131
Let's land this.

237
00:18:40,471 --> 00:18:42,131
You should now be able to say:

238
00:18:42,471 --> 00:18:46,822
The private key never leaves the machine;
GitHub holds the public key; a fingerprint

239
00:18:46,862 --> 00:18:48,285
is a hash of the public key.

240
00:18:48,625 --> 00:18:53,340
The agent holds decrypted keys in memory;
the passphrase protects the file on disk.

241
00:18:53,680 --> 00:19:00,426
~/.ssh/config maps a host or alias to a
host name, user git, port and key, first

242
00:19:00,466 --> 00:19:05,176
value wins; ssh -G prints the
result without connecting.

243
00:19:05,516 --> 00:19:10,219
A host key mismatch is a published rotation
or an attack; the published fingerprints

244
00:19:10,259 --> 00:19:12,478
decide; the check is never switched off.

245
00:19:12,818 --> 00:19:20,159
ssh -T git@github.com tells you which
account the server sees; ssh.github.com on

246
00:19:20,199 --> 00:19:24,680
port 443 is the fallback
when port 22 is blocked.

247
00:19:26,520 --> 00:19:30,143
Read sections 16.8 to 16.12 of Chapter 16.

248
00:19:30,483 --> 00:19:35,363
Today you followed an SSH connection through
both proofs, and you repaired a host

249
00:19:35,403 --> 00:19:40,343
key error without switching the check off.
That's a lot of moving parts, so if one

250
00:19:40,383 --> 00:19:45,202
of the five steps still blurs, replay its
demo. Next time: two identities on one

251
00:19:45,242 --> 00:19:48,481
machine, credentials for
machines, and single sign-on.

252
00:19:48,521 --> 00:19:51,640
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

253
00:19:51,680 --> 00:19:53,097
See you in the next one.
